tools/pipeline/ledger.json-25194-   "witness": "harness:regress/artifacts/panelseatlabel/panel_20260824_231824/SEATS.txt -- *** RE-CITED 2026-09-11 TO A DURABLE PATH.  THE FIELD SAID: \"none needed -- both are visible in _scratchpad/panel_20260824_231824/SEATS.txt\" ***  That path is at the PROJECT ROOT, one level ABOVE the git root, and `git ls-files _scratchpad` returns zero -- so the citation pointed outside the repo and a reader resolving it from the repo root lands in a DIFFERENT `GXEMUL-SEC/_scratchpad/` that does not contain it.  All 15 files copied here, evidence verified present first.  The term is also corrected from `none needed`: the round's subject is `panel.sh` and its seat check, i.e. the test apparatus, which is exactly what `harness:` is admitted for.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:25195:   "state_note": "*** BOTH DEFECTS WERE ALREADY FIXED BY `faffad7` AND THIS ROW SAT OPEN. Verified 2026-08-26 AGAINST LIVE OUTPUT, not by reading the source. *** Today's panel printed:\n\n    *** WALLED / RATE-LIMITED / MISCONFIGURED -- NOT a considered non-answer: kimi\n    *** read each seat file for the ACTUAL status; do not assume 429.\n    ... the kimi CLI is walled but kimi-k3:cloud ANSWERED -- that seat\n        is COVERED by its substitute transport, not lost.\n\n**(1) is discharged**: the label no longer names a status code the script did not observe, and `panel.sh:554-560` carries a comment stating the exact defect this row filed -- a 403 billing-cycle wall printed as `HTTP 429`. **(2) is discharged**: `:565-566` reports a CLI wall with a live substitute as ONE SEAT ANSWERED rather than one lost, which is what the row asked for.\n\n*** THIS IS THE SECOND FIXED-DEFECT-RECORDED-AS-OPEN FOUND TODAY, after `sh4dmacie` (fixed by `f532bb6`, recorded `held` with no commit). *** The cost is not cosmetic: an open row is an offer of work to a fresh session, so a stale one spends a round rediscovering that there is nothing to do -- and it inflates the queue that everything else is prioritised against.\n\n**The check that found it was cheap and is worth repeating: for a tooling row, run the tool and read what it prints.** Reading the source would have worked here too, but running it is faster and cannot be fooled by a fix that was written and never wired -- which is exactly the failure `#465` shipped with its detector.\n\nMEASURED on `_scratchpad/panel_20260824_231824`. Two separate reporting defects in the seat check, neither of which changes a disposition but both of which mislead a later reader. (1) The kimi CLI failed with `provider.auth_error: 403 ... usage limit for this billing cycle` and SEATS.txt printed `*** RATE-LIMITED (HTTP 429)`. The carrier draws a sharp line between failure classes -- a WALL you wait out versus a REFUSED CONNECTION one command fixes -- and a status code named wrongly is how that line gets blurred. (2) It printed `SEATS: 6/7 scriptable` while `ollama_kimi.txt` (17,382 bytes) sat answered in the same directory: the check counts the CLI seat only, so a seat that is walled on one transport and LIVE on another is tallied purely as a loss. That undercounts the panel exactly when the substitution is working. Note the related counting trap the carrier already records: the two Agent seats leave NO FILE, so any file-based count under-reports by two more.  *** THE THREE CLI SEATS HAVE DIFFERENT WRITE BEHAVIOURS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM MAKES A SIZE CHECK MEANINGFUL MID-RUN. Measured 2026-08-25 on the #463 records audit. *** `codex` STREAMS its reasoning, so its file grows continuously and was 311 KB while still working. `grok` streams slowly -- 229 then 357 bytes, each sample a partial sentence. **`agy` writes NOTHING until it finishes**: its file sat at EXACTLY 0 bytes for minutes with a live `agy.exe` (pid 23548), then lands in one shot -- 8,044 bytes on the pass-2 panel. So for agy, and ONLY for agy, a zero-byte file is the normal mid-run state, and it is indistinguishable BY SIZE from the plain-`&` failure the carrier records (where a killed child leaves a zero-byte log). **The discriminator is the PROCESS, not the file**: `tasklist | grep agy` settles it in one command. Note the related trap already recorded -- check `tasklist` WITHOUT `/v`, because `/v` changes the column layout and a follow-up check using it once reported no processes when three were alive, nearly destroying a true positive.  *** AND THE THIRD DEFECT IS IN THE FIX: A WALL-KEYWORD CHECK PRODUCES A FALSE POSITIVE ON ANY SEAT THAT READS THIS LEDGER. Measured 2026-08-25. *** After widening the seat check to catch the quota/403/429/stalled family, it scored a LIVE codex seat as WALLED. The seat was mid-answer at 225 KB and still running; what the check matched was **this project's own prose about walls** -- the rows describing a 403 mislabelled as 429, and the empty-response class -- echoed back by a seat that had been asked to read the ledger. **A keyword detector applied to a document ABOUT that keyword cannot work**, and the tasks most likely to trip it are exactly the records and matrix audits this project now runs routinely. THE DISCRIMINATOR IS POSITION AND TERMINATION, not presence -- which is what the carrier already says (`verify the CONTENT, never the size`) applied one level down: a walled seat's notice is the LAST thing in the file with nothing substantive after it, and the answer either ends mid-sentence or is the echoed brief. A live seat mentions a wall in the middle of a large body of its own reasoning and keeps going. `seat_verdicts.py` already uses a POSITION discriminator for exactly this reason; the seat check should borrow it rather than grep the whole tail. Cheapest interim guard, and it costs one line: **if the process is still alive, it is not walled** -- `tasklist` settles it, and this project has now used that test twice in one session (once to prove a 0-byte agy file was buffering rather than dead).  *** SECOND MEASURED INSTANCE 2026-08-25 (`panel_20260825_112845`), AND THE SECOND DEFECT IS WORSE THAN THIS ROW SAYS -- IT IS NOT A TALLY PROBLEM, IT IS A LOST SEAT. *** Defect (1) reproduced exactly: the kimi CLI returned `provider.auth_error: 403 You've reached your usage limit for this billing cycle` and SEATS.txt printed `*** RATE-LIMITED (HTTP 429)`. Same wrong status code, same blurring of the wall-versus-transport line the carrier draws. *** BUT DEFECT (2) IS NOT MERELY AN UNDERCOUNT. *** This row records that the tally omits a substitute transport that ANSWERED. Measured today: `panel.sh` **never fires the substitute at all**. It launches exactly one kimi seat -- the CLI, at `:222` -- and its three Ollama seats are hardcoded to `$OLLAMA1/2/3` (glm, deepseek, minimax) at `:270/:274/:278`. Meanwhile `curl -s localhost:11434/api/tags` lists **`kimi-k3:cloud` as LIVE**, and three earlier panels (`panel_20260824_191634`, `panel_20260824_231824`, `panel_20260825_000248`) each hold an `ollama_kimi.txt`, so the seat was being fired BY HAND and then stopped being. *** SO THE HONEST STATUS OF THIS SEAT IS NOT \"DOWN\". *** kimi's CLI transport is walled; kimi itself is reachable and idle. Every panel since the CLI wall has silently run one seat short while a working transport sat unused -- which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape with an extra step, because here the roster does not even record a failure to investigate. RECOVERED IMMEDIATELY: the seat was fired by hand onto the live `wdcflood` pass-1 panel dir as `ollama_kimi.txt`. FIX OWED IN panel.sh, deliberately NOT APPLIED YET because panel.sh was RUNNING when this was found and bash reads a script incrementally -- editing a running script is its own defect class. The fix: fire `kimi-k3:cloud` as a fourth Ollama seat, and have the seat check report the CLI wall and the substitute SEPARATELY so a walled transport with a live substitute reads as one seat answered, not as a loss. Also correct `429` to the actual status text rather than a hardcoded code. *** AND THE GENERAL RULE THIS EARNS: WHEN A SEAT WALLS, ASK WHETHER THE MODEL IS REACHABLE BY ANOTHER TRANSPORT BEFORE RECORDING IT DOWN. *** The carrier already separates a wall from a refused connection; this is the third class -- **one transport walled, another live** -- and it is the only one where the seat is available the whole time and nobody asks.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json-25196-   "note": "*** 2026-09-11 VOLATILE-PATH CHECK.  **THE PATH STILL EXISTS -- AND IT IS WORSE THAN `_scratchpad/`, IT IS OUTSIDE THE REPO ENTIRELY.** ***\n\nThis row's witness reads *'none needed -- both are visible in `_scratchpad/panel_20260824_231824/SEATS.txt`'*.  **The file is there** -- 685 bytes, mtime 2026-08-24 23:33 -- and it does prove both halves of the claim: `*** RATE-LIMITED (HTTP 429), NOT a considered non-answer: kimi` against a 403 wall (the 328-byte `kimi.txt` sits beside it), and `SEATS: 6/7 scriptable` while a 17,382-byte `ollama_kimi.txt` sits in the same directory unrecorded.\n\n> *** BUT THE DURABILITY PROBLEM IS SHARPER THAN 'IT IS IN SCRATCHPAD'.  `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` is `GXEMUL-SEC/`, and the cited `_scratchpad/` is at the PROJECT ROOT -- ONE LEVEL ABOVE THE REPO.  `git ls-files _scratchpad` returns ZERO tracked files. ***\n\n**And there is a SECOND, different `GXEMUL-SEC/_scratchpad/` inside the repo** -- it contains a file literally named `README_THIS_IS_NOT_THE_SCRATCHPAD.md` -- **and the cited panel dir is not in it.**  *So a future reader resolving this citation from the repo root lands in the wrong directory and finds nothing, which reads as 'the evidence is gone' rather than 'you are one level too low'.*\n\n**This is the `wdcoddstep` situation exactly**, which this project has already recorded: a precedent-setting row was *'one `ls` away from being unverifiable'* while durable copies existed all along.  **The remedy is the same and costs one `cp`: copy the directory to `regress/artifacts/panelseatlabel/` before the citation is relied on again.**  *Not done in this pass -- the row is CLOSED, and the same audit's disposition is FILE rather than sweep closed rows; but unlike the witness-term corrections this one is a LOSS RISK rather than a vocabulary error, so it is called out separately.*"
